        "AcceptedAnswerId": "553",
        "AnswerCount": "3",
        "Body": "&lt;p&gt;We got &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/314/can-we-get-mathjax-enabled&quot;&gt;MathJax enabled&lt;/a&gt;. Today I learned that there is even more! We could have &lt;a href=&quot;https://worldbuilding.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/9710/can-we-extend-mhchem-support-in-mathjax-to-include-physical-units?cb=1&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;support for Physical units&lt;/strong&gt; enabled as part of the mhchem package&lt;/a&gt;! We use physical units &lt;strong&gt;a lot&lt;/strong&gt; and demand the SI formatting. Let me quote from Worldbuilding:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Chemistry Stack Exchange is obviously the main consumer of this feature and has a &lt;a href=&quot;https://worldbuilding.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/9710/can-we-extend-mhchem-support-in-mathjax-to-include-physical-units?cb=1&quot;&gt;nice FAQ on its use&lt;/a&gt;. The chemists get two things that we don't:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;\\require{mhchem}&lt;/code&gt; is implicit... you get support automatically without having to require it explicitly.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;They get physical unit formatting via &lt;code&gt;$\\pu{273.15 K}$&lt;/code&gt;, which currently renders as a grumpy error on WB.SE and WB meta that looks [with only mhchem enabled] like this &lt;a href=&quot;https://i.sstatic.net/NyXoU.png&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.sstatic.net/NyXoU.png&quot; alt=&quot;mathjax markup error&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can we have that too? Pretty please?&lt;/strong&gt; I am tired of having to type &lt;code&gt;$273.15\\ \\text{K}$&lt;/code&gt; and such, especially if it is complex things in the unit!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;",
        "CommentCount": "5",
        "ContentLicense": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "CreationDate": "2022-05-08T19:12:53.590",
        "Id": "537",
        "LastActivityDate": "2022-08-04T17:23:50.920",
