        "@tail": null,
        "@text": "",
        "Body": "&lt;p&gt;Seen that all three tags are all used on three questions (not separately) a single tag would have been more than enough. &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/input-shaping&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;input-shaping&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;input-shaping&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-input-shaping-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;input-shaping&lt;/a&gt; would be sufficient.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If others would be using other tags, we could then decide to merge the tags for synonyms.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;",
        "CommentCount": "1",
        "ContentLicense": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "CreationDate": "2023-11-21T13:53:52.753",
        "Id": "581",
        "LastActivityDate": "2023-11-21T22:49:50.167",
        "LastEditDate": "2023-11-21T22:49:50.167",
        "LastEditorUserId": "5740",
        "OwnerUserId": "5740",
        "ParentId": "580",
        "PostTypeId": "2",
        "Score": "1"
      },
      {
        "": "row",
        "-": "posts",
        "--": null,
        "@index": 0,
        "@tail": null,
        "@text": "",
        "Body": "&lt;p&gt;The problem is that there are more volatile other media then there used to be, e.g. Reddit, but the quality of the answers there is generally disappointing. There is so much more information, video channels and fora to be found that each has their own dedicated group of users.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;SE tries to aim for high quality (community) moderator driven question and answer sites, this just doesn't addresses everybody, sometimes people want to go into endless discussions for which SE is not the platform.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The analytics page (&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/help/privileges/site-analytics&quot;&gt;visible for 5000 rep users&lt;/a&gt;, but a history is maintained fro a while in &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/a/265/&quot;&gt;What does it take to get out of Beta stage?&lt;/a&gt;) shows that the site gets frequently hit, many first timers that are drawn here through search engine queries.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do note that 3D printing is in effect a pretty simple process, like an internal combustion engine needs air, fuel and a spark, a 3D printer needs a location, filament heating and preprocessed file; after a while most questions are asked. Appearently, the users find their answers in the existing questions. New techniques and advances in firmware and software trigger new questions like recently we receive more Klipper, input shaping and Resin related print questions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Migration &amp;quot;from and to&amp;quot; other sites does happen but not frequent. The provided links do show many topics related to 3D printing, but looking at the questions, the vast majority does not belong here according to our &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/help/on-topic&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;quot;What topics can I ask about here?&amp;quot;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;",
        "CommentCount": "2",
        "ContentLicense": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "CreationDate": "2023-11-22T19:21:34.553",
        "Id": "583",
        "LastActivityDate": "2023-11-23T12:08:47.977",
        "LastEditDate": "2023-11-23T12:08:47.977",
        "LastEditorUserId": "5740",
        "OwnerUserId": "5740",
        "ParentId": "582",
        "PostTypeId": "2",
        "Score": "2"
      },
      {
        "": "row",
        "-": "posts",
        "--": null,
        "@index": 0,
        "@tail": null,
        "@text": "",
        "Body": "&lt;h3&gt;TL;DR&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Similar concerns about the size of the community have been raised for many years, as the links below show.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr /&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Migration&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;h4&gt;General&lt;/h4&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It is worth remembering that &lt;em&gt;migration&lt;/em&gt; only has a rather small window (of a few days or so) after which a question is deemed &amp;quot;too old to migrate&amp;quot; and the option just greyed out (or unavailable, I forget which now)... annoying I know, but those are the rules.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;IMHO, migration is pointless (and can require &amp;quot;back-channel&amp;quot; communication and agreements between sites) and just not worth the bother. Generally, it is much more simpler and efficient (and rapid) to just ask the poster to delete their question on the first site and just re-post it themselves on the new site.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;em&gt;very&lt;/em&gt; similar question was asked recently on the Robotics Meta, please read &lt;a href=&quot;https://robotics.meta.stackexchange.com/q/1475/9176&quot;&gt;Can/should questions on Stack Overflow about ROS be migrated here?&lt;/a&gt;. The &lt;a href=&quot;https://robotics.meta.stackexchange.com/a/1476/9176&quot;&gt;answer&lt;/a&gt; seems to also apply to this site and (more or less) re-iterates what I said above.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackexchange.com/a/137931/280335&quot;&gt;this answer&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/136537/280335&quot;&gt;Is &amp;quot;Too old to migrate&amp;quot; a good reason NOT to migrate?&lt;/a&gt; is really useful.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h4&gt;Other sites&lt;/h4&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I looked at some of the &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/search?q=gcode&quot;&gt;G-code questions on SO&lt;/a&gt; and a number of them are related to simply programmatic issues, parsing issues or substitution, that don't have a 3D Printing end-game, and so they wouldn't be on-topic anyway.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href=&quot;https://robotics.stackexchange.com/&quot;&gt;Robotics site&lt;/a&gt; was in a real lull until quite recently, when the &lt;a href=&quot;https://robotics.meta.stackexchange.com/q/1501/9176&quot;&gt;ROS (Q&amp;amp;A) community was merged&lt;/a&gt;. In the past some 3D printing questions &lt;em&gt;were&lt;/em&gt; posted there, and I &lt;em&gt;did&lt;/em&gt; think about getting those migrated, but then the OP had a meltdown and rage-quit, so that was the end of that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href=&quot;https://engineering.stackexchange.com/&quot;&gt;Engineering site&lt;/a&gt; also has some 3D design/modelling questions that &lt;em&gt;could&lt;/em&gt; have fitted here, but (as has been &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackexchange.com/questions/80216/can-i-ask-the-same-question-on-more-than-one-stack-exchange-site#:%7E:text=Ask%20on%20the%20site%20you,is%20a%20very%20bad%20idea.&quot;&gt;stated elsewhere&lt;/a&gt;) it is OK for some overlap to exist between SE sites, a question might be suited to &lt;em&gt;more than one site&lt;/em&gt; and it doesn't really matter where it lands - the question just gets slightly re-jigged for the appropriate audience.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Traffic&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;There are graphs and spreadsheets and databases that can be queried to give you this sort of info. Early numbers can be seen in &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/a/265/4762&quot;&gt;this (now rather ramshackle) answer&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/264/4762&quot;&gt;What does it take to get out of Beta stage?&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also (vaguely) related are questions discussing participation:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/511/4762&quot;&gt;New users aren&amp;#39;t posting their own questions - are we scaring them off?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;more to be added as they are found...&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h4&gt;A subjective view&lt;/h4&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have had the (possibly unjustified) &lt;em&gt;feeling&lt;/em&gt; that the site has tailed off since early 2022 or so (as I noted at the end of &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/a/557/4762&quot;&gt;this answer&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/556/4762&quot;&gt;Is 3dprinting.stackexchange.com likely to still be active in the foreseeable future?&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My &lt;em&gt;overall impression&lt;/em&gt; is that this site was vibrant and had at least 10+ permanently active users, seven years ago, back in 2016. The questions back then seemed to be more related to:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;building DIY printers;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;fixing Prusa printers and their clones;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;filament queries;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;general Arduino questions, and;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;firmware issues.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, 3D printing has become much more mainstream, in the past few years, there are many more reliable manufacturers of printers that (apparently) just &amp;quot;work out of the box&amp;quot; and less people are building from scratch. So, less questions... apart from the initial flurry of Creality support questions&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;, when these printers first started to become popular (4-5 years ago?).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Nevertheless, even these types of questions seem to have tailed off, now, after a &lt;em&gt;subsequent&lt;/em&gt; flurry of questions about Creality add-ons, such as the BLTouch (et al).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, w.r.t. repair, as 0scar states, most of the tinkering and repairing questions have already been asked and answered, back in the days of DIY printers. However, now and again a good old-fashioned board repair question comes along that you can get your teeth into, but they are becoming increasingly infrequent.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Nowadays, we &lt;em&gt;seem&lt;/em&gt; to be down to a core of 4 or 5 regular visitors and  a few other less-regular users.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h5&gt;Chat room&lt;/h5&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've just remembered that we also have a vibrant chat room (&lt;em&gt;two&lt;/em&gt; in fact, see &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/307/4762&quot;&gt;Do we have a chatroom?&lt;/a&gt;), which were active in 2018 onwards, and (after having just checked) the &lt;a href=&quot;https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/79830/public-3d-printing-room&quot;&gt;Public 3D Printing chatroom&lt;/a&gt; still is.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Promotion&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;There was the opportunity to advertise our site, using community ads, but I'm not sure what ever came of that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;See:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/76/community-ads-lets-make-2d-ads-for-ourselves&quot;&gt;Community Ads! Let&amp;#39;s make 2d ads for ourselves!&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/291/which-se-sites-should-we-target-with-community-ads&quot;&gt;Which SE sites should we target with community ads?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/384/4762&quot;&gt;Advertising on other SE sites: 2019 Version&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, this question has (sort of) been asked before, &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/74/4762&quot;&gt;How do we get more traffic to the site?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Voting&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; raise a good point: No votes makes it hard to &lt;em&gt;sort the wheat from the chaff&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Again, the voting (or apparent lack thereof) has been raised in the past, see:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/176/how-do-we-motivate-users-to-vote-and-advertise-the-voting-policy&quot;&gt;How do we motivate users to vote and advertise the voting policy?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/30/vote-early-vote-often&quot;&gt;Vote early, Vote often&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/196/remember-to-vote&quot;&gt;Remember to vote!&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Although, all of these seem to be from 2016. I really thought that there were a couple more recently asked questions... but maybe there aren't.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr /&gt;&#xA;&lt;h4&gt;Footnote&lt;/h4&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; See the tail end of &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/a/328/4762&quot;&gt;this answer&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/264/what-does-it-take-to-get-out-of-beta-stage/265#comment802_328&quot;&gt;this comment&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;",
        "CommentCount": "0",
        "ContentLicense": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "CreationDate": "2023-11-23T15:56:25.373",
        "Id": "584",
        "LastActivityDate": "2023-11-28T16:21:45.510",
        "LastEditDate": "2023-11-28T16:21:45.510",
        "LastEditorUserId": "4762",
        "OwnerUserId": "4762",
        "ParentId": "582",
        "PostTypeId": "2",
        "Score": "3"
      },
      {
        "": "row",
        "-": "posts",
        "--": null,
        "@index": 0,
        "@tail": null,
        "@text": "",
        "Body": "&lt;p&gt;I can't speak for the other reviewers of your suggested edits, but...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Possibly, at first glance at your links, I would avoid just changing the title into a question (from an already grammatically correct statement) on an &lt;em&gt;old established&lt;/em&gt; question - as this adds little to the post and just bumps the question (again).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;From your examples: #1, #2, #4 and #5 just seem to change a couple of words, but with little (or no) added value - apart from making a grammatical question, which isn't really strictly necessary. Many sites use title statements rather than questions - to be honest, either seem acceptable, so just changing titles (of old questions) into grammatically correct interrogations seems unnecessary. You could do that for the new posts, as they aren't yet established - but if you are trying to look up an older question and the title has been changed, then that could be taken as &amp;quot;making the post more confusing&amp;quot;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, there are &lt;em&gt;a lot&lt;/em&gt; of question titles that aren't questions (but statements instead), and converting them &lt;em&gt;all&lt;/em&gt; would mean a lot of suggested edit reviews - all for little to no gain.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Focusing on #5 for a moment, adding the &amp;quot;Longer LK5 Pro&amp;quot; to the title is &lt;em&gt;redundant&lt;/em&gt; as the question is already tagged with &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/longer-lk5&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;longer-lk5&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;longer-lk5&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-longer-lk5-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;longer-lk5&lt;/a&gt;. Generally, when a question is tagged with &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;something&lt;/em&gt;&amp;quot;, that &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;something&lt;/em&gt;&amp;quot; is then &lt;em&gt;removed&lt;/em&gt; from the title due to redundancy (see the section &lt;strong&gt;Avoid putting tags in the question title&lt;/strong&gt; under &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackoverflow.com/a/303220/4424636&quot;&gt;this answer&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackoverflow.com/q/303219/4424636&quot;&gt;How do I make a good edit?&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That said, your example #3 does seem useful as it does (admittedly) describe the issue better. That was one that I rejected, purely because (IIRC) it is an old question that already has an answer (that probably should have been accepted, had the user returned to the site after posting the question).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Actually, sticking with #3, the &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;with TMC2208 and Klipper&lt;/em&gt;&amp;quot; in the title should be removed as it is redundant, due to the tags (going by what was said above), which (by those rules) would leave the title as just:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Creality Sprite Extruder custom install on Ender 3 Pro&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;which &lt;em&gt;really&lt;/em&gt; is &lt;em&gt;utterly meaningless&lt;/em&gt;! LOL. :-) So, maybe the &lt;em&gt;part&lt;/em&gt; of your suggested edit that includes the voltage is a very good idea - even though the answer then states that the voltages should be ignored - so something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How to configure the TMC2208 Driver Voltage for a Creality Sprite Extruder custom install&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and maybe tag with &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/ender-3-pro&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender-3-pro&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender-3-pro&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-ender-3-pro-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;ender-3-pro&lt;/a&gt;, if there is one, otherwise the existing &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/ender-3&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender-3&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender-3&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-ender-3-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;ender-3&lt;/a&gt; tag might suffice..? However, I'm not sure how the Ender printers are meant to be tagged, and I might be wrong. There was a Meta post/discussion somewhere by Trish and/or 0scar about a possible hierarchy of tags, i.e. something &lt;em&gt;like&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/creality&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;creality&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;creality&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-creality-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;creality&lt;/a&gt; and&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/ender&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;ender&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-ender-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;ender&lt;/a&gt; and then &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/3&quot; class=&quot;post-tag&quot; title=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;3&amp;#39;&quot; aria-label=&quot;show questions tagged &amp;#39;3&amp;#39;&quot; rel=&quot;tag&quot; aria-labelledby=&quot;tag-3-tooltip-container&quot;&gt;3&lt;/a&gt;, which I think was rejected - however, I've had a good look and really can't find the meta post.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, in summary, I would reject all of those edits again, apart from #3 as the title is a bit of a mess anyway - remove the redundant already tagged info from the title and mention the stepper driver voltages. If you suggest that edit, I'll accept it :-)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Suggestions&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As this is a small site (with little traffic, as discussed in your &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/582/4762&quot;&gt;previous meta post&lt;/a&gt;), most of the questions have been edited to death already and as such, it can be difficult to find things to fix. It is probably best to stick to &lt;strong&gt;new posts&lt;/strong&gt; that haven't yet been edited, or if an older post is blatantly incorrect and has been missed then that should be fixed.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You might find this useful: &lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/500/4762&quot;&gt;Useful things to check for when cleaning up posts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I notice that you've added some tags in other suggested edits. Many of these tags have no description, which can make it a bit harder to review the suggested edit, w.r.t the validity of the tag.  If you could edit such &amp;quot;empty&amp;quot; tags, with descriptions and usage, that would be &lt;em&gt;really&lt;/em&gt; useful. In which case, these are related:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/565/4762&quot;&gt;Do we have a template for a tag excerpt?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://3dprinting.meta.stackexchange.com/q/116/4762&quot;&gt;Circular tag wiki excerpts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr /&gt;&#xA;&lt;h4&gt;The problem with mods doing reviews&lt;/h4&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As there really are &lt;em&gt;so few&lt;/em&gt; regular users on the site, the review queues often fall to the moderators - otherwise they would never be cleared.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One issue with mods undertaking reviews, is that we are &lt;em&gt;unable&lt;/em&gt; to vote as normal users, see &lt;a href=&quot;https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/41062/280335&quot;&gt;Add a way for moderators to cast a normal, non binding close/open vote&lt;/a&gt;. That means that only one vote of ours is needed, which &lt;em&gt;seems&lt;/em&gt; very undemocratic. I'm not happy about this, and would much prefer to have a community of votes, as is the usual case.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, we don't have that luxury, and so some votes may seem autocratic in nature. However, as you have done, bringing up any perceived slight is always a good idea, and raising them here in Meta is always good for a discussion.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;",
        "CommentCount": "3",
        "ContentLicense": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "CreationDate": "2023-11-27T16:12:58.447",
        "Id": "586",
        "LastActivityDate": "2023-11-27T22:09:22.380",
        "LastEditDate": "2023-11-27T22:09:22.380",
        "LastEditorUserId": "4762",
        "OwnerUserId": "4762",
        "ParentId": "585",
        "PostTypeId": "2",
